<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <img src="placeholder_irs_logo.png" alt="IRS Logo" style="height: 50px;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 24pt; margin-top: 10px;">2025</h1>
    <h2 style="font-size: 18pt;">INSTRUCTIONS FOR SCHEDULE H</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Household Employment Taxes</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Section references are to the Internal Revenue Code unless otherwise noted.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Future Developments</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <p>For the latest information about developments related to Schedule H and its instructions, such as legislation enacted after they were published, go to <a href="#">IRS.gov/ScheduleH</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>What's New</h3>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Social security and Medicare taxes for 2025.</strong> The social security tax rate is <span data-field="ss_tax_rate">{{ ss_tax_rate }}</span>% for the employee and employer. The social security wage base is <span data-field="ss_wage_base">{{ ss_wage_base }}</span>.</p>
  <p>The Medicare tax rate is <span data-field="medicare_tax_rate">{{ medicare_tax_rate }}</span>% for the employee and employer, unchanged from 2024. There is no wage base limit for Medicare tax.</p>
  <p>Basic social security and Medicare taxes apply to the wages of household workers you pay. <span data-field="wage_base_info">{{ wage_base_info }}</span>. For more information, see <a href="#">Cash wages</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Qualified parking exemption and commuter transportation benefit.</strong> For 2025, the monthly exclusion for qualified parking is <span data-field="parking_exclusion">{{ parking_exclusion }}</span> and the monthly exclusion for commuter transportation is <span data-field="commuter_exclusion">{{ commuter_exclusion }}</span>.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Withholding on qualified overtime compensation.</h3>
  </div>
  <p>For 2025, the overtime tax rate is <span data-field="overtime_tax_rate">{{ overtime_tax_rate }}</span>. <span data-field="overtime_details">{{ overtime_details }}</span>.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Reminders</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>The COVID-19 related credit for qualified sick and family leave wages is limited to leave taken after March 31, 2020, and before October 1, 2021, and may not be claimed on Schedule H (Form 1040).</strong></p>
    <p>The time periods for providing the leave for the credits for qualified sick and family leave wages, as enacted under the Families First Coronavirus Response Act (FFCRA) and amended and extended by the COVID-related Tax Act of 2021, are as follows: March 1, 2020, through December 31, 2020, and before April 1, 2021, and the credit for qualified sick and family leave wages under sections 3131, 3132, and 3133 of the Internal Revenue Code, as enacted under the American Rescue Plan Act of 2021 (the ARP), for leave taken after March 31, 2021, and before October 1, 2021.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Household work is done in or around your home.</h3>
  </div>
  <p>The following sections describe the types of workers who do household work:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Babysitters</li>
    <li>Cooks</li>
    <li>Maids</li>
    <li>Nannies</li>
    <li>Caregivers</li>
    <li>Health aides</li>
    <li>Privates</li>
    <li>Housekeepers</li>
    <li>Yard workers</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>References to federal income tax withholding</h3>
  </div>
  <p>For more information on the different types of work, see Section 16 of Pub. 15.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>References to federal income tax withholding</h3>
  </div>
  <p>For more information on the different types of work, see Section 16 of Pub. 15.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>General Instructions</h3>
  </div>
  <p>These instructions give you some background information about Schedule H. They tell you who must file Schedule H, how to complete it line by line, and when and where to file it. If you want more in-depth information about household employment tax topics relating to Schedule H, see Pub. 525.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Who Needs to File Schedule H?</h3>
  </div>
  <p>You must file Schedule H if you answer "Yes" to any of the questions on lines A, B, and C of Schedule H.</p>
  <p><strong>Did you have a household employee?</strong> If you hired someone to do household work and you could control what work they did and how they did it, you had a household employee. This is true even if you gave the employee freedom of action. What matters is that you had the right to control the details of how the work was done.</p>
  <p><strong>Example.</strong> You paid Peyton O to babysit your child and do light housework 4 days a week in your home. Peyton is your household employee.</p>
  <p><strong>Example.</strong> You paid a cleaning service to clean your home. The cleaning service is your employer. Peyton is your household employee.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Do You Have an Employer Identification Number (EIN)?</h3>
  </div>
  <p>If you have household employees, you will need an EIN to file Schedule H. If you don't have an EIN, you may apply for one online by going to <a href="#">IRS.gov/EIN</a>. You may also apply for an EIN by faxing or mailing Form SS-4 to the IRS. Don't use your social security number (SSN) in place of an EIN. Instructions for Form SS-4 are in Pub. 1474.</p>
  <p>You can get an EIN immediately over the Internet. If you apply by fax or mail, it may take several weeks to receive it. You may apply by mail. Go to <a href="#">IRS.gov/Forms</a> to get forms and publications, including Form SS-4.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Can Your Employee Legally Work in the United States?</h3>
  </div>
  <p>It is unlawful to employ a person who can't legally work in the United States. When you hire a household employee, you must make sure that the person is legally authorized to work in the United States and you must keep Form I-9 for your employees. You must verify the employee's identity and employment authorization. You can do this by completing Form I-9, Employment Eligibility Verification. You must keep Form I-9 for the entire period of employment. You must keep Form I-9 for all household employees.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Filing Form W-2 and Form W-3</h3>
  </div>
  <p>You're encouraged to file Forms W-2 and W-3 electronically. Go to the SSA's Employer's W-2 Filing Instructions & Information website at <a href="#">SSA.gov/employer</a>.</p>
  <p>If you file Forms W-2 and W-3 electronically, don't mail the forms. You can also file them by paper. For more information, see <a href="#">SSA.gov/employer</a>.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Where to file papers Form W-2 and W-3.</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Copy a copy of Form W-2 with Form W-3 attached with your tax return.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Social Security Administration</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Direct Operations Center</p>
  <p>Wikes-Bare, PA 18769-0001</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>TIP</h3>
  </div>
  <p>If you use Certified Mail to file, change the ZIP code to <span data-field="zip_code">{{ zip_code }}</span>. Please deliver Form W-2 to the address and change the ZIP code to <span data-field="zip_code_2">{{ zip_code_2 }}</span> for a list of IRS-approved PO Boxes.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Specific Instructions</h3>
  </div>
  <p><strong>How To Fill in Schedule H, Form W-2, and Form W-3</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Completing Schedule H</strong></p>
  <p>If you were notified that your household employee received compensation, you must file Schedule H. If you are attaching Schedule H to Form 1040, 1040-SR, or 1040-SS, your name must match the name shown on your return. Only two Schedules H can be attached to Form 1040.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Part I. Household Employment Taxes</h3>
  </div>
  <p>From line 8 on Schedule 2 (Form 1040), line 9. If you don't use Form 1040, complete Schedule H, Part I, and follow the instructions under When and Where To File.</p>
  <p>If the total for any quarter in 2024 or 2025 is $1,000 or more, check "Yes" and complete Schedule H, Part I.</p>
  <p>Together with state unemployment taxes, the FUTA tax provides funds for paying unemployment compensation to workers who have lost their jobs. Most employers pay both a federal and a state unemployment tax.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Part II. Federal Unemployment (FUTA) Tax</h3>
  </div>
  <p>FUTA tax applies to the first $7,000 you pay to each employee during a calendar year after subtracting any payments exempt from FUTA. The FUTA tax rate is <span data-field="futa_rate">{{ futa_rate }}</span>%. Only employers who pay federal unemployment tax must pay FUTA tax. You must pay FUTA tax on wages up to the FUTA wage base.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>State Unemployment Taxes</h3>
  </div>
  <p>State unemployment taxes sometimes called contributions. Contributions are payments that a state makes to fund unemployment benefits. However, contributions don't include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Any payments deducted or reimbursed from your employee's pay.</li>
    <li>Voluntary contributions you paid to get a lower state unemployment tax rate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you paid contributions to any credit reduction state, see the instructions for line 23.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Lines 10 through 12.</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Answer the questions on lines 10 through 12 to see if you should complete Section A or Section B of Part II.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Section A</h3>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Line 13.</strong> Name of the state where you paid unemployment contributions. If you paid unemployment contributions to more than one state, list all states. For a list of states and their postal abbreviations, see <a href="#">State Names and Postal Abbreviations</a>.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Part III. Total Household Employment Taxes</h3>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Line 25.</strong> Enter the amount from line 8. Enter the amount from line 16 (or line 24) and line 25. Add the amounts on lines 16 (or line 24) and line 25. Add the amounts on lines 24 and 25 and enter the total on line 26.</p>
  <p><strong>Line 27.</strong> Are you required to file Form 1040? Follow the instructions in the following chart.</p>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <td style="width: 50%;">If you file Form...</td>
      <td style="width: 50%;">THEN enter the amount from line 26 on Schedule H, Part I, line 9.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1040 or 1040-SR</td>
      <td>Schedule 2 (Form 1040), line 9.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1040-NR</td>
      <td>Form 1040-SR, Part I, line 9.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1041</td>
      <td>Form 1041, Schedule G, Part I, line 7.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <img src="placeholder_irs_logo.png" alt="IRS Logo" style="height: 50px;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 24pt; margin-top: 10px;">TREASURY/IRS AND OMB USE ONLY DRAFT</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Part II. Federal Unemployment (FUTA) Tax</h3>
  </div>
  <p>If you don't file any of the above forms, complete Schedule H, Part II, and follow the instructions under When and Where To File.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>When and Where To File</h3>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Exceptions.</strong> If you get an extension of time to file your return, file your return with Schedule H by the due date of your fiscal year return, not by the extended due date.</p>
  <p>If you file a calendar year taxpayer and have no FUTA tax liability, you do not have to file Schedule H for 2025.</p>
  <p>If you have household employees for 2025, you don't have to file Schedule H for 2025.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Section B</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Complete lines 17 through 24 only if you checked "No" on line 10, 11, or 12.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Credit for 2025.</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Credit for 2025. The credit you can take for any state unemployment fund contributions for 2025 that you paid to your household employees is limited to the amount of the contributions you paid to your household employees for the calendar year in which services were performed.</p>
  <p>You must complete columns (a), (b), and (h), even if you have no credit. If you have no credit, you must still complete the columns as if you had a credit. If you were given a rate of less than 5.4%, you must complete all columns.</p>
  <p>If you were given a rate of 5.4% or higher, you must also complete columns (c) and (d).</p>
  <p>If you were given a rate of less than 5.4%, you do not have to complete columns (c) and (d).</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Column (h). Contributions paid to state unemployment funds.</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Enter the total contributions paid to state unemployment funds for the calendar year 2025. If you are claiming excess credits as payments to your household employees, attach a copy of the letter from your state.</p>

  <div class="section-header">
    <h3>Paid Preparers</h3>
  </div>
  <p>Paid Preparer Use Only. You must complete this part if you were paid to prepare Schedule H. If you are not paid to prepare Schedule H, then do not complete this part.</p>
  <p>If you file one or more Forms W-2 and W-3, you must also file Form 941, Employer's Quarterly Federal Tax Return, if you are an employer.</p>
  <p>You must report both cash and noncash wages in box 1 of Form 941, Employer's Quarterly Federal Tax Return. You must also report the total wages in box 1 of Form 941, Employer's Quarterly Federal Tax Return.</p>
  <p>For detailed information on preparing Forms W-2 and W-3, see the General Instructions for Forms W-2 and W-3.</p>
  <p>For more information on filing Forms W-2 and W-3 electronically, go to the SSA's Employer's W-2 Filing Instructions & Information website at <a href="#">SSA.gov/employer</a>.</p>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px;">
